Keep a directory listing whole while entries are created and removed in it.

ntfs3 hands out readdir positions that are byte offsets inside an index
block, and walks the blocks in the order they sit on disk. Removing an entry
moves the ones after it down, and rebalancing moves entries between blocks, so
a position handed out before the change points past entries nobody has seen.
Finder deletes a folder as it lists it, and ends with "some items had to be
skipped" and files left behind.

A position now names the entry the listing goes on from, by its MFT record and
a hash of its name, and the listing walks the index in name order. Going on
from a position looks its name up again: in the inode when it is in memory, in
the MFT record when not, and, when the entry has since been removed, among the
last 4096 names removed from that directory, which each directory now keeps.
Whatever was created or removed meanwhile, nothing is skipped and nothing is
returned twice. A position older than those 4096 is logged and the listing
starts again, the one case left in which an entry can come twice.

The index is walked here and not with indx_find_sort(), which reads a subnode
into the node it came from and frees nodes without their buffers. Directories
get their own llseek, positions reaching past s_maxbytes.

